      "name": "request_clarification",
      "input": {
        "question": "Do the input files include the aligned reads (BAM/CRAM from STAR) alongside the count matrix, or only counts.csv and metadata.csv as listed in the data profile?",
        "questionContext": "The research question explicitly flags that strandedness was not recorded and asks me to verify \"the counts are right.\" The rigorous check for this — RSeQC infer_experiment (or an equivalent read-vs-annotation strand inference) — requires the aligned BAM files and a gene model (BED/GTF), because it reads the mapped-read orientation relative to annotated exons. The persisted data profile lists only two input files: counts.csv (a gene x sample integer count matrix) and metadata.csv (sample table). No BAM/CRAM/SAM alignment files are listed, and no gene annotation (GTF/BED) is provisioned in the reference store I can see (I found chrom.sizes, EnsDb.Hsapiens.v86, Reactome/MSigDB/WikiPathways gene sets, STRING PPI, and UniProt ID mapping, but no exon/gene-model BED/GTF that RSeQC-style tools need, and RSeQC itself is not installed in the sandbox).\n\nGiven that, I need to know: (a) are alignment files actually available somewhere for this analysis that the profile didn't list, so a step could run a direct strand inference on them, or (b) should the plan instead treat strandedness as unrecoverable from the count matrix alone and proceed with count-level diagnostics only (e.g., flag any genes with substantial antisense/overlapping-gene ambiguity is not directly testable from counts.csv; the closest we can do is a downstream biological plausibility check — inspecting known strand-marker/antisense gene pairs or comparing to public liver expression as a sanity check — and explicitly document strandedness as an unresolved, stated limitation of the analysis rather than something the plan claims to fix)?\n\nThis changes a central step: if alignments exist, I'd add a QC step to run strand inference on the BAM files before trusting the counts; if only the count matrix is available (as the profile currently states), that step is not executable and I would instead plan the analysis with strandedness explicitly flagged as an unverifiable caveat, addressed only by a plausibility/sanity check on the counts, not a definitive inference."
      }
    }
